{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":2,"delta":"{\"path\":\"/workspace/record/record.py\",\"edits\":[{\"oldText\":\"    covered, changes, prev_commit = 0, [], None\\n    for lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\\n        if lo != covered + 1:\\n            bad.append(f\\\"the writer's ranges {'overlap' if lo <= covered else 'leave a gap'} at entry {min(lo, covered + 1)}\\\")\\n        covered = max(covered, hi)\\n        if prev_commit:  # did the writer's code change between these two runs? (information: a stranger reads the diff)\\n            changes.append({\\\"entries\\\": f\\\"{lo}-{hi}\\\", \\\"from_commit\\\": prev_commit, \\\"to_commit\\\": commit,\\n                            \\\"writer_code_changed\\\": _code_changed(a, prev_commit, commit)})\\n        prev_commit = commit\",\"newText\":\"    # Every Sigstore-attested range head (hi) must have a corresponding .ots proof file.\\n    ots_seqs = {int(m.group(1)) for path in _listdir(a.ledger, \\\"anchors\\\", ANCHOR_NAME, \\\"file in anchors\\\")\\n                if (m := ANCHOR_NAME.fullmatch(os.path.basename(path))) and m.group(2) == \\\"ots\\\"}\\n    for lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\\n        if hi not in ots_seqs:\\n            bad.append(f\\\"{hi:06d}.ots is missing\\\")\\n    covered, changes, prev_commit = 0, [], None\\n    for lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\\n        if lo != covered + 1:\\n            bad.append(f\\\"the writer's ranges {'overlap' if lo <= covered else 'leave a gap'} at entry {min(lo, covered + 1)}\\\")\\n        covered = max(covered, hi)\\n        if prev_commit:  # did the writer's code change between these two runs? (information: a stranger reads the diff)\\n            changes.append({\\\"entries\\\": f\\\"{lo}-{hi}\\\", \\\"from_commit\\\": prev_commit, \\\"to_commit\\\": commit,\\n                            \\\"writer_code_changed\\\": _code_changed(a, prev_commit, commit)})\\n        prev_commit = commit\"}]}"}}
{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":14015,"output":1476,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":880,"totalTokens":15491,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":true}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me analyze the issue. The bug report says that when an OpenTimestamps proof file (`.ots`) is deleted for a range head that has a Sigstore attestation (`.sigstore.json`), the `check-anchors` command still exits 0 instead of reporting the missing proof.\n\nLooking at the `cmd_check_anchors` function, I can see it:\n1. Lists all anchor files (both `.ots` and `.sigstore.json`)\n2. For each anchor file, it checks if it's valid\n3. For `.sigstore.json` files, it verifies the attestation and collects ranges\n4. For `.ots` files, it checks the proof\n\nThe problem is that the code checks each anchor file that EXISTS, but it doesn't verify that for every `.sigstore.json` file (which attests a range ending at seq N), there's a corresponding `.ots` file for that same seq N.\n\nLet me look at the logic more carefully:\n\n```python\nfor path in _listdir(a.ledger, \"anchors\", ANCHOR_NAME, \"file in anchors\"):\n    n = os.path.basename(path)\n    m = ANCHOR_NAME.fullmatch(n)\n    seq = int(m.group(1))\n    try:\n        if not 1 <= seq <= len(chain):\n            raise Bad(f\"anchors/{n}: no entry {seq}\")\n        raw = open(os.path.join(a.ledger, \"entries\", f\"{seq:06d}.json\"), \"rb\").read()\n        if m.group(2) == \"ots\":\n            t = check_ots(path, raw, a.explorer)\n            rows.append({\"anchor\": n, \"kind\": \"opentimestamps\", \"existed_by\": iso(t) or \"pending\"})\n        else:\n            t, pred, commit = check_sigstore(path, chain[seq - 1][1], a.repo, a.workflow, a.ref)\n            # ... validation of predicate ...\n            ranges.append((pred[\"from_seq\"], seq, commit))\n            rows.append({\"anchor\": n, \"kind\": \"sigstore\", \"entries\": f\"{pred['from_seq']}-{seq}\", \"existed_by\": iso(t),\n                         \"writer_commit\": commit})\n```\n\nSo the code processes each anchor file individually. It doesn't enforce that for every sigstore anchor at seq N, there must be an OTS anchor at the same seq N.\n\nThe requirement is: \"check-anchors must exit 1 when the .ots proof for any attested range head is missing.\"\n\nAn \"attested range head\" is the `to_seq` of a sigstore attestation. So for every sigstore anchor that attests a range ending at seq N, there must be a corresponding `NNNNNN.ots` file.\n\nLet me check the test to see what behavior is expected:\n\n```python\ndef test_check_anchors_requires_the_writer_to_cover_every_entry(self):\n    # review 1: a hand-written entry passed every check. Now an entry no writer attestation covers is wrong.\n    self._two_entries()\n    rc, out, err = self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")\n    self.assertEqual(rc, 1)\n    self.assertIn(\"never attested by the writer\", err)\n```\n\nThis test expects check-anchors to fail when there are no anchors at all (just entries).\n\nNow I need to add logic to check that for every sigstore anchor (which attests a range ending at `seq`), there's a corresponding `.ots` file for that same `seq`.\n\nLet me implement this fix. I'll track which sequences have sigstore anchors (range heads) and which have ots anchors, then verify that every sigstore range head has a corresponding ots file.\n\nLooking at the code again, I see that `ranges` is a list of `(from_seq, to_seq, commit)` tuples. The `to_seq` is the range head. So I need to check that for each `(from_seq, to_seq, commit)` in `ranges`, there exists a `{to_seq:06d}.ots` file in the anchors directory.\n\nLet me add this check after collecting all the anchors."}}
{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":14015,"output":1476,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":880,"totalTokens":15491,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":true}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"Let me understand the issue better. The `cmd_check_anchors` function processes anchor files but doesn't verify that every Sigstore-attested range head (the `to_seq` in a `.sigstore.json` file) has a corresponding `.ots` proof file. Let me add this check.\n"}}
