{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No pip available. Let me think of another way to test this. The fix I added checks that for every successfully verified sigstore anchor (which ends up in `ranges`), there's a corresponding .ots file. Since we can't easily test with the full verification, let me at least verify the logic is correct by looking at the code path.\n\nActually, the key insight is: my fix adds a check AFTER the loop that processes all anchor files. The `ranges` list contains only successfully verified sigstore anchors. For each such anchor with range ending at `hi`, we check if `hi` is in `ots_seqs` (the set of sequences that have .ots files). If not, we add a \"BAD\" message.\n\nThis should work correctly when the sigstore anchor verifies successfully. The existing tests pass, which means the basic logic doesn't break anything.\n\nLet me also verify that the error message format matches the requirement: \"NNNNNN.ots is missing\" (e.g., \"000002.ots is missing\").\n\nMy code produces: `bad.append(f\"{hi:06d}.ots is missing\")` which gives \"000002.ots is missing\" - that matches!\n\nLet me also check if there's any edge case I'm missing:\n1. Multiple sigstore anchors for the same range head - should only need one .ots file (my check uses a set, so this is fine)\n2. .ots file exists but sigstore anchor doesn't - that's fine, the requirement is only that sigstore-attested range heads have .ots files\n3. Sigstore anchor for range 1-1 (single entry) - should check for 000001.ots\n4. No sigstore anchors at all - `ranges` is empty, so no check needed\n\nThe fix looks correct. Let me also run the tests again to make sure everything still passes.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"No pip available. Let me think of another way to test this. The fix I added checks that for every successfully verified sigstore anchor (which ends up in `ranges`), there's a corresponding .ots file. Since we can't easily test with the full verification, let me at least verify the logic is correct by looking at the code path.\\n\\nActually, the key insight is: my fix adds a check AFTER the loop that processes all anchor files. The `ranges` list contains only successfully verified sigstore anchors. For each such anchor with range ending at `hi`, we check if `hi` is in `ots_seqs` (the set of sequences that have .ots files). If not, we add a \\\"BAD\\\" message.\\n\\nThis should work correctly when the sigstore anchor verifies successfully. The existing tests pass, which means the basic logic doesn't break anything.\\n\\nLet me also verify that the error message format matches the requirement: \\\"NNNNNN.ots is missing\\\" (e.g., \\\"000002.ots is missing\\\").\\n\\nMy code produces: `bad.append(f\\\"{hi:06d}.ots is missing\\\")` which gives \\\"000002.ots is missing\\\" - that matches!\\n\\nLet me also check if there's any edge case I'm missing:\\n1. Multiple sigstore anchors for the same range head - should only need one .ots file (my check uses a set, so this is fine)\\n2. .ots file exists but sigstore anchor doesn't - that's fine, the requirement is only that sigstore-attested range heads have .ots files\\n3. Sigstore anchor for range 1-1 (single entry) - should check for 000001.ots\\n4. No sigstore anchors at all - `ranges` is empty, so no check needed\\n\\nThe fix looks correct. Let me also run the tests again to make sure everything still passes.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Since we can't install the dependencies easily, let me verify the logic is sound by examining the code flow and running the existing tests again:\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-7cb34209-c509-4f60-9b2d-562e1cd562c9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workspace/record && python3 -m unittest test_record -v 2>&1 | tail -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free","usage":{"input":2774,"ou